<% consultation_url = PublicProductRscDemoPresenter::CONSULTATION_URL %>
<% react_on_rails_github_url = PublicProductRscDemoPresenter::REACT_ON_RAILS_GITHUB_URL %>
<% react_on_rails_pro_url = PublicProductRscDemoPresenter::REACT_ON_RAILS_PRO_URL %>
<% shakapacker_github_url = PublicProductRscDemoPresenter::SHAKAPACKER_GITHUB_URL %>

<div class="dd dd-performance">
  <main class="dd-main">
    <header class="dd-header dd-hero-grid">
      <div>
        <p class="dd-eyebrow">VP Engineering brief</p>
        <h1>RSC is the stronger bounded public-page candidate</h1>
        <p>
          On matched stable Product and Discover fixtures, the React on Rails Pro RSC routes nearly halve browser
          navigation time and JavaScript while reducing total HTML plus JavaScript by 41&ndash;45%. They send more
          initial HTML, and server response completion is about the same.
        </p>
        <p class="dd-proof-line">
          <strong>Recommendation:</strong> continue to a production-parity pilot after React on Rails Pro 17 final.
          Keep Inertia as the control and rollback path, and gate rollout on mobile field Web Vitals, availability,
          and equivalent production chrome and analytics.
        </p>
      </div>

      <div class="dd-actions">
        <a href="<%= rsc_performance_demo_path %>#current-shakaperf-result" class="dd-btn">Read the detailed ShakaPerf evidence</a>
        <a href="<%= public_product_rsc_demo_path %>" class="dd-btn">Open Product RSC</a>
        <a href="<%= public_product_discover_rsc_demo_path %>" class="dd-btn">Open Discover RSC</a>
      </div>
    </header>

    <section class="dd-evidence-strip" aria-label="Executive performance evidence">
      <article class="dd-evidence-card">
        <p class="dd-eyebrow">Product navigation</p>
        <h2><%= @executive_summary.dig(:product, :navigation_delta) %></h2>
        <p>Matched stable-deployment Inertia control to RSC candidate.</p>
      </article>
      <article class="dd-evidence-card">
        <p class="dd-eyebrow">Discover navigation</p>
        <h2><%= @executive_summary.dig(:discover, :navigation_delta) %></h2>
        <p>Matched stable-deployment Inertia control to RSC candidate.</p>
      </article>
      <article class="dd-evidence-card">
        <p class="dd-eyebrow">Total HTML + JavaScript</p>
        <h2><%= @executive_summary.dig(:product, :total_wire_delta) %> / <%= @executive_summary.dig(:discover, :total_wire_delta) %></h2>
        <p>Product / Discover. The lower total is driven by about half the JavaScript and one route script instead of nine.</p>
      </article>
    </section>

    <div class="dd-body">
      <section class="dd-section">
        <header>
          <h2>Benefits worth taking to a pilot</h2>
        </header>
        <div class="dd-list">
          <article class="dd-row"><p class="dd-copy"><strong>Faster matched navigation:</strong> both public surfaces improved in every paired cycle.</p></article>
          <article class="dd-row"><p class="dd-copy"><strong>Less client work:</strong> JavaScript transfer fell about 49.5%, combined-median decoded JavaScript plus CSS fell 53.2%, and route scripts fell from nine requests to one.</p></article>
          <article class="dd-row"><p class="dd-copy"><strong>Bounded adoption:</strong> Product and Discover can move independently while Inertia remains the control and rollback path.</p></article>
        </div>
      </section>

      <section class="dd-section">
        <header>
          <h2>Costs and tradeoffs</h2>
        </header>
        <div class="dd-list">
          <article class="dd-row">
            <p class="dd-copy">
              <strong>More initial HTML:</strong> Product adds <%= @executive_summary.dig(:product, :html_cost) %> and
              Discover adds <%= @executive_summary.dig(:discover, :html_cost) %>. The absolute increase is modest,
              but the relative increase is 80&ndash;100%.
            </p>
          </article>
          <article class="dd-row"><p class="dd-copy"><strong>No demonstrated server-response win:</strong> Product is <%= @executive_summary.dig(:product, :response_end) %>; Discover is <%= @executive_summary.dig(:discover, :response_end) %>.</p></article>
          <article class="dd-row"><p class="dd-copy"><strong>Operational surface:</strong> React on Rails Pro, the Node renderer, isolated bundles, observability, and production fallback add ownership and deployment complexity.</p></article>
        </div>
      </section>

      <section class="dd-section">
        <header>
          <h2>What is open source and what is commercial</h2>
          <p>
            An unanswered licensing question quietly kills adoption, so here is the exact split for this demo.
          </p>
        </header>
        <div class="dd-list">
          <article class="dd-row">
            <p class="dd-copy">
              <strong>MIT open source:</strong> this Gumroad demo codebase, the
              <a href="<%= react_on_rails_github_url %>" target="_blank" rel="noopener">react_on_rails</a> gem, and
              <a href="<%= shakapacker_github_url %>" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Shakapacker</a>. The matched
              Inertia control routes run on open source only; <code>inertia_rails</code> is MIT as well.
            </p>
          </article>
          <article class="dd-row">
            <p class="dd-copy">
              <strong>Commercial:</strong> the RSC candidate routes additionally use
              <a href="<%= react_on_rails_pro_url %>" target="_blank" rel="noopener">React on Rails Pro</a> &mdash;
              the Pro gem and npm package, the Node renderer, and <code>react-on-rails-rsc</code> &mdash; under the
              ShakaCode EULA. Production use requires a paid subscription or a complimentary open-source license.
            </p>
          </article>
          <article class="dd-row">
            <p class="dd-copy">
              <strong>For this specific case:</strong> Gumroad is an MIT-licensed open-source application, and the Pro
              EULA includes a complimentary one-year renewable license track for qualifying open-source organizations,
              issued at ShakaCode&rsquo;s discretion, so a bounded pilot does not have to start with a purchase
              decision. Rolling a route back to the Inertia control removes the commercial dependency entirely.
              <a href="<%= consultation_url %>" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Ask ShakaCode</a> which track applies.
            </p>
          </article>
        </div>
      </section>

      <section class="dd-section">
        <header>
          <h2>Evidence quality and production gate</h2>
          <p>
            Confidence is high that this deployed end-to-end candidate beats the matched Inertia control on navigation
            and client payload. Confidence is not yet high for production Web Vitals: the current run is unthrottled
            desktop Chrome, uses synthetic media, and does not match live Gumroad chrome, fonts, caching, or third parties.
          </p>
        </header>
        <p class="dd-note is-warning">
          Lower client JavaScript should improve production responsiveness and may improve loading Web Vitals,
          especially on mobile. That is a hypothesis, not a demonstrated production result. Validate it with
          production-shaped media, equivalent analytics and chrome, and real-user LCP and INP after React on Rails Pro
          17 final.
        </p>
      </section>

      <div class="dd-actions">
        <a href="<%= rsc_performance_demo_path %>#current-shakaperf-result" class="dd-btn">Read the detailed ShakaPerf evidence</a>
        <a href="<%= rsc_performance_demo_path %>#reproduce-with-shakaperf" class="dd-btn">Review the rollout gates</a>
        <a href="<%= consultation_url %>" target="_blank" rel="noopener" class="dd-btn">Book a ShakaCode consultation</a>
      </div>
    </div>
  </main>
</div>
